%I A398689 #75 Oct 29 2026 15:08:02
%S A398689 5,7,7,8,9,9,9,10,9,10,10,10
%N A398689 Packing total chromatic number of the cycle graph C_n.
%C A398689 A packing total coloring of a graph G assigns positive integer colors to its vertices and edges such that any two distinct elements (vertices or edges) assigned color i have distance greater than i in the total graph T(G). The packing total chromatic number is the minimum number of colors required.
%C A398689 Ferme and Mesarič Štesl established a(3) through a(13) in Theorem 7 of their paper.
%C A398689 Musachio (2026) established a(14) = 10 by an explicit ten-coloring and a combinatorial proof that nine colors are insufficient.
%C A398689 For n >= 3, T(C_n) is isomorphic to the square of the cycle C_{2n}.
%H A398689 Jasmina Ferme and Daša Mesarič Štesl, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.08691">On packing total coloring</a>, arXiv:2508.08691 [math.CO], 2025-2026.
%H A398689 Daniel Musachio, <a href="/A398689/a398689.pdf">The Packing Total Chromatic Number of the 14-Cycle</a>.
%e A398689 a(3) = 5. The total graph T(C_3) is isomorphic to C_6^2. Assigning the colors (1, 2, 3, 1, 4, 5) to consecutive vertices of C_6^2 gives an optimal packing coloring. The two vertices colored 1 have distance 2, and all other colors occur exactly once. Four colors are insufficient since at most two vertices can receive color 1, while each color i >= 2 can occur at most once.
%K A398689 nonn,new
%O A398689 3,1
